**Problem.** Find all entire functions $f(z)$ such that $|f(z)|=1$ whenever $|z|=1$.

**Answer.** $f(z)=c\,z^{n}$ where $|c|=1$ and $n\in\{0,1,2,\dots\}$.

---

**Proof.**

**Step 1 (Boundedness inside the disk).** Since $f$ is entire it is continuous on the closed unit disc $\overline{\mathbb D}$, and $|f|=1$ on $\partial\mathbb D$. By the maximum modulus principle,
$$|f(z)|\le 1\qquad(|z|\le 1). \tag{1}$$

**Step 2 (A reflection identity).** Define, for $z\neq 0$,
$$\Phi(z)\;=\;\overline{f(1/\bar z)}\;=\;\sum_{k\ge 0}\overline{a_k}\,z^{-k},\qquad f(z)=\sum_{k\ge 0}a_k z^k.$$
The series $\sum \overline{a_k}z^{-k}$ converges for every $z\neq 0$ (because $f$ is entire), so $\Phi$ is analytic on $\mathbb C\setminus\{0\}$. On $|z|=1$ we have $1/\bar z = z$, hence
$$\Phi(z)=\overline{f(z)}=\frac{1}{f(z)}\qquad(|z|=1),$$
using $|f(z)|^{2}=1$. Therefore the product
$$H(z):=f(z)\,\Phi(z)=f(z)\,\overline{f(1/\bar z)}$$
is analytic on $\mathbb C\setminus\{0\}$ (entire times analytic-off-$0$) and equals $1$ on the unit circle. Since the unit circle has accumulation points and $H$ is analytic in a neighbourhood of every point of it, the identity theorem gives
$$\boxed{\,f(z)\,\overline{f(1/\bar z)}=1\quad\text{for all }z\neq 0\,}. \tag{2}$$

**Step 3 (No non-zero zeros inside the disc).** Suppose $f(a)=0$ with $0<|a|<1$. Set $z=1/\bar a$ (so $|z|>1$ and $1/\bar z = a$) in (2):
$$f(1/\bar a)\,\overline{f(a)}=f(1/\bar a)\cdot 0 = 0 = 1,$$
a contradiction. Hence the only possible zero of $f$ in $\mathbb D$ is at the origin.

**Step 4 (Factor out the zero at the origin).** Let $n\ge 0$ be the order of the zero of $f$ at $0$ ($n=0$ means $f(0)\neq 0$). Write
$$f(z)=z^{n}g(z),$$
with $g$ entire and $g(0)\neq 0$. By Step 3, $g$ has **no** zeros in $\overline{\mathbb D}$: on $\partial\mathbb D$, $|g|=|f|/|z|^{n}=1\neq 0$; in $\mathbb D$, every zero of $g$ would be a non-origin zero of $f$, which is impossible; and $g(0)\neq 0$.

**Step 5 ($g$ is constant).** Since $g$ has no zeros in $\overline{\mathbb D}$, the function $1/g$ is analytic on $\overline{\mathbb D}$, and $|1/g|=1$ on $\partial\mathbb D$. By the maximum modulus principle applied to $1/g$,
$$|1/g(z)|\le 1\quad\text{in }\mathbb D\quad\Longrightarrow\quad |g(z)|\ge 1\quad\text{in }\mathbb D. \tag{3}$$
On the other hand, applying the maximum modulus principle to $g$ (using $|g|=1$ on $\partial\mathbb D$),
$$|g(z)|\le 1\quad\text{in }\mathbb D. \tag{4}$$
Together, (3) and (4) give $|g|\equiv 1$ in $\mathbb D$, so $g$ is constant on the disc (open mapping theorem: a non-constant analytic map is open, but $g(\mathbb D)\subseteq\partial\mathbb D$ has empty interior). Being entire and constant on an open set, $g\equiv c$ with $|c|=1$.

**Step 6 (Conclusion).** Thus
$$f(z)=c\,z^{n},\qquad |c|=1,\quad n\in\{0,1,2,\dots\}.$$

**Verification.** Every such function is entire, and on $|z|=1$,
$$|f(z)|=|c|\,|z|^{n}=1\cdot 1=1.$$

These are exactly all entire functions satisfying the condition. $\blacksquare$

